'''Classical Nahuatl''', also known simply as ''Aztec'' or ''Nahuatl'', is a set of variants of [[Nahuatl]] spoken in the [[Valley of Mexico]] and central Mexico as a ''[[lingua franca]]'' at the time of the 16th-century [[Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire]]. Indigenous languages, including Classical Nahuatl, remained dominant until the time when [[New Spain]] (Mexico) became independent in 1821.<ref>{{Citation |last=de León |first=Lourdes |title=Indigenous Language Policy and Education in Mexico |date=2017 |work=Language Policy and Political Issues in Education |pages=415–433 |url=https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-319-02344-1_31 |access-date=2026-04-11 |publisher=Springer, Cham |language=en |doi=10.1007/978-3-319-02344-1_31 |isbn=978-3-319-02344-1|url-access=subscription }}</ref> However, during the subsequent centuries, it was largely displaced by [[Spanish language|Spanish]] and evolved into some of the modern [[Nahuan languages]] in use (other modern dialects descend more directly from other 16th-century variants). Although classified as an [[extinct language]],<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.ethnologue.com/show_language.asp?code=nci |title=Ethnologue summary for Classical Nahuatl |access-date=2006-06-09 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130218111931/http://www.ethnologue.com/show_language.asp?code=nci |archive-date=2013-02-18 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Classical Nahuatl has survived through a multitude of written sources transcribed by [[Nahua peoples]] and Spaniards in the Latin script.

Classical Nahuatl may be called ''Codical Nahuatl'' (when referring to the variants employed in the [[Mesoamerican codices]] through the medium of the [[Aztec script]]) or ''Colonial Nahuatl'' (if written in post-conquest documents in the [[Latin script|Latin alphabet]]).

==Classification==
Classical Nahuatl is one of the [[Nahuan languages]] within the [[Uto-Aztecan languages|Uto-Aztecan family]]. It is classified as a central dialect and is most closely related to the modern dialects of Nahuatl spoken in the valley of Mexico in colonial and modern times. It is probable that the Classical Nahuatl documented by 16th- and 17th-century written sources represents a particularly prestigious [[sociolect]]. That is to say, the variety of Nahuatl recorded in these documents is most likely to be more particularly representative of the speech of Aztec nobles ({{lang|nci|[[pipiltin|pīpiltin]]}}), while the commoners ({{lang|nci|[[Macehualtin|mācēhualtin]]}}) spoke a somewhat different variety.

